<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 9</p>
  <h1>Question tags: <em>build the grammar, then choose the social signal</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">A question tag adds a small checking phrase to a statement: <span class="ex">You sent the link, didn’t you?</span> The statement carries your current understanding. The tag asks the listener to confirm, correct, or align with it. Accurate grammar comes from the statement’s operator and subject; conversational meaning comes from context, voice movement, and how much agreement you seem to expect.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">statement + operator + pronoun</span>
    <span class="tag">polarity · response · intonation</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Separate the statement from its checking tag.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Find the operator, reverse polarity, and track the subject.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Listen</b><span>Hear expected agreement or a more open check.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Confirm a fictional event plan without pressuring agreement.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can build standard opposite-polarity tags with <span class="ex">be</span>, perfect <span class="ex">have</span>, modals, and <span class="ex">do</span>; use contemporary U.S. <span class="ex">do</span>-support with lexical <span class="ex">have</span>; replace nouns and indefinite subjects with the right tag pronoun; treat <span class="ex">never</span>, <span class="ex">nobody</span>, <span class="ex">nothing</span>, and <span class="ex">hardly</span> as negative in meaning; handle <span class="ex">I’m ..., aren’t I?</span>, <span class="ex">Let’s ..., shall we?</span>, and common imperative tags; answer according to the truth of the statement; use rising or falling intonation as a context-sensitive social signal; and choose a full question when a tag could pressure the listener.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered be, perfect have, and modal can as the operators that will reappear in their matching tags.">
    <h3>Reactivate the statement operators</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Question tag foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is">is</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="can">can</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="does">does</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="did">did</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>In <span class="ex">Lena is ready</span>, the visible operator is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is" data-hint="The statement uses present be before the adjective ready." data-fix="The operator is is.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>In <span class="ex">They have finished</span>, the Present Perfect operator is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have" data-hint="Have carries tense before the participle finished." data-fix="The Present Perfect operator is have.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>In <span class="ex">Marcus can help</span>, the modal operator is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can" data-hint="The modal comes directly before base help." data-fix="The modal operator is can.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice one event plan and two different checking jobs</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Cedar House event board, 4:00 p.m.</h3>
    <p><b>Confirmed line:</b> Main speaker arrives at 5:30.</p>
    <p><b>Blank task:</b> Send the guest Wi-Fi code.</p>
    <p><b>Known fact:</b> Printed handouts are not required.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Lena: “The speaker arrives at 5:30, doesn’t she?”</span> <b>Falling voice:</b> Lena reads a confirmed line and expects Marcus to agree.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Marcus: “You haven’t sent the Wi-Fi code, have you?”</span> <b>Rising voice:</b> The board is blank, and Marcus genuinely needs to check.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Lena: “No, I haven’t. Let’s send it now, shall we?”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which statement is positive, and which tag is negative?</li>
      <li>Which statement is negative, and which tag is positive?</li>
      <li>Does <span class="ex">No, I haven’t</span> confirm or correct the negative statement?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You reversed polarity, repeated the matching operator, interpreted No as confirmation of the negative statement, and linked each voice movement to its stated context.">
    <h3>Discovery check: form and conversational job</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Question tag discovery choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="negative">negative</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="does">does</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="positive">positive</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="confirms that the code has not been sent">confirms that the code has not been sent</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a more open check">a more open check</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The statement <span class="ex">The speaker arrives at 5:30</span> is positive, so <span class="ex">doesn’t she</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="negative" data-hint="The contraction doesn’t contains not." data-fix="Doesn’t she is a negative tag.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The lexical Present Simple verb <span class="ex">arrives</span> requires tag operator <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="does" data-hint="There is no visible auxiliary, and the subject speaker is third-person singular." data-fix="The tag uses does: doesn’t she?">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The statement <span class="ex">You haven’t sent the code</span> is negative, so <span class="ex">have you</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="positive" data-hint="The tag has no not after have." data-fix="Have you is a positive tag.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Lena’s reply <span class="ex">No, I haven’t</span> <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="confirms that the code has not been sent" data-hint="The short answer agrees with the negative statement’s truth." data-fix="No, I haven’t confirms that the code has not been sent.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Marcus’s rising voice matches the supplied context because he makes <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a more open check" data-hint="The blank board does not tell him whether Lena completed the task elsewhere." data-fix="The rising tag functions as a more open check here.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: a tag echoes the statement’s operator</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>An <b>operator</b> is the first auxiliary or modal that carries tense, polarity, or question behavior. If the statement already contains <span class="ex">be</span>, perfect <span class="ex">have</span>, or a modal, echo that operator in the tag. Reverse positive and negative polarity, then replace the statement subject with a matching pronoun.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>statement system</th><th>operator echoed in tag</th><th>complete example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>copular or auxiliary <span class="ex">be</span></td><td><span class="ex">is / are / was / were</span></td><td><span class="ex">Lena is ready, isn’t she?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Present Perfect</td><td><span class="ex">have / has</span></td><td><span class="ex">They’ve finished, haven’t they?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>modal or future</td><td>same modal</td><td><span class="ex">Marcus can help, can’t he? The talk will start soon, won’t it?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>lexical Present or Past Simple</td><td><span class="ex">do / does / did</span></td><td><span class="ex">She works here, doesn’t she? They called, didn’t they?</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>With more than one auxiliary, repeat the first operator: <span class="ex">They will have finished, won’t they?</span> Contractions require meaning, not spelling alone. <span class="ex">He’s the speaker, isn’t he?</span> contains <span class="ex">he is</span>; <span class="ex">He’s arrived, hasn’t he?</span> contains <span class="ex">he has</span>.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You echoed be, perfect have, and the first modal, and you resolved he’s from the complement that followed it.">
    <h3>Operator check: repeat the system already present</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Question tag operator choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="isn’t she">isn’t she</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are they">are they</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="haven’t you">haven’t you</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="won’t they">won’t they</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="isn’t he">isn’t he</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="hasn’t he">hasn’t he</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Positive <span class="ex">be</span> statement: Lena is ready, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="isn’t she" data-hint="Echo is, make it negative, and use she for Lena." data-fix="Lena is ready, isn’t she?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Negative <span class="ex">be</span> statement: The guests aren’t waiting outside, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are they" data-hint="Echo are positively and use they for the guests." data-fix="The guests aren’t waiting outside, are they?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Positive Present Perfect: You’ve paid the caterer, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="haven’t you" data-hint="You’ve means you have before the participle paid." data-fix="You’ve paid the caterer, haven’t you?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Two auxiliaries; echo the first one: They will have finished by five, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="won’t they" data-hint="The first operator is will, so the negative tag is won’t they." data-fix="They will have finished by five, won’t they?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span><span class="ex">He’s</span> before the noun phrase <span class="ex">the main speaker</span> means <span class="ex">he is</span>: He’s the main speaker, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="isn’t he" data-hint="The noun phrase after he’s identifies him, so echo is." data-fix="He’s the main speaker, isn’t he?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span><span class="ex">He’s</span> before participle <span class="ex">arrived</span> means <span class="ex">he has</span>: He’s arrived, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="hasn’t he" data-hint="Arrived is the participle in the Present Perfect, so echo has." data-fix="He’s arrived, hasn’t he?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Supply <span class="ex">do</span> for lexical Present and Past Simple</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>When the statement has a lexical Present or Past Simple verb and no visible operator, supply the same <span class="ex">do</span>-support used in ordinary questions: <span class="ex">Lena works here, doesn’t she?</span> and <span class="ex">Marcus called, didn’t he?</span> The tag does not repeat the main verb.</p>
  <p>In contemporary U.S. English, lexical possession with <span class="ex">have</span> normally uses <span class="ex">do</span>: <span class="ex">You have the room key, don’t you?</span> Perfect <span class="ex">have</span> is different: <span class="ex">You’ve checked the room, haven’t you?</span> British and other varieties may also use <span class="ex">You have a car, haven’t you?</span>; the controlled choices here target the stated U.S. pattern, not a universal ban.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You supplied does for singular present, do for plural and U.S. lexical have, and did for Past Simple while leaving the main verb out of the tag.">
    <h3>Do-support check: no visible operator in the statement</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Do support tag choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="doesn’t she">doesn’t she</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="don’t they">don’t they</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="didn’t he">didn’t he</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="don’t you">don’t you</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="did it">did it</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Third-person singular Present Simple: Lena works at Cedar House, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="doesn’t she" data-hint="Supply negative does and use she for Lena." data-fix="Lena works at Cedar House, doesn’t she?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Plural Present Simple: The doors open at five, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="don’t they" data-hint="Supply negative do and use they for the doors." data-fix="The doors open at five, don’t they?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Positive Past Simple: Marcus sent the schedule yesterday, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="didn’t he" data-hint="Supply negative did and use he for Marcus." data-fix="Marcus sent the schedule yesterday, didn’t he?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Contemporary U.S. lexical possession: You have the room key, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="don’t you" data-hint="This have means possess, so the target U.S. pattern uses do-support." data-fix="You have the room key, don’t you?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Negative Past Simple: The confirmation email didn’t arrive, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="did it" data-hint="Reverse negative did to positive did and use it for the email." data-fix="The confirmation email didn’t arrive, did it?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Track the subject reference into a pronoun</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A standard tag normally uses a pronoun rather than repeating the full noun. People and things take their ordinary reference pronouns. Indefinite people such as <span class="ex">everyone, someone, no one</span>, and <span class="ex">nobody</span> commonly take singular <span class="ex">they</span> in the tag. <span class="ex">This, that</span>, and <span class="ex">nothing</span> take <span class="ex">it</span>; <span class="ex">these</span> and <span class="ex">those</span> take <span class="ex">they</span>. Existential <span class="ex">there</span> stays <span class="ex">there</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You replaced a named person and plural noun, used singular they for everyone, changed this to it, and preserved existential there.">
    <h3>Reference check: who or what does the tag point back to?</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Question tag subject choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="she">she</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="they">they</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="it">it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="there">there</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Lena has the guest list, doesn’t <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="she" data-hint="Lena is one woman, so the tag pronoun is she." data-fix="Lena has the guest list, doesn’t she?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The caterers have arrived, haven’t <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="they" data-hint="The plural people caterers become they." data-fix="The caterers have arrived, haven’t they?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Everyone is ready, aren’t <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="they" data-hint="Everyone is grammatically singular in the statement but commonly takes singular they in the tag." data-fix="Everyone is ready, aren’t they?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>This is the final version, isn’t <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="it" data-hint="This normally becomes it in a standard tag." data-fix="This is the final version, isn’t it?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>There are enough chairs, aren’t <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="there" data-hint="Existential there remains there in the tag." data-fix="There are enough chairs, aren’t there?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Read negative meaning, not only visible <span class="ex">not</span></h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Words such as <span class="ex">never, nobody, no one, nothing, hardly, rarely, seldom</span>, and <span class="ex">scarcely</span> make the statement negative in meaning. They normally take a positive tag: <span class="ex">Nobody called, did they?</span> Do not add a negative tag just because there is no visible <span class="ex">not</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recognized negative meaning in nobody, nothing, never, and hardly, then formed a positive tag with the matching operator and pronoun.">
    <h3>Meaning check: the negative may be inside another word</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Negative meaning tag choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="did they">did they</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is it">is it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="does it">does it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="do they">do they</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Nobody called the speaker, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="did they" data-hint="Nobody makes the statement negative; use positive did and singular they." data-fix="Nobody called the speaker, did they?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Nothing is missing, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is it" data-hint="Nothing is negative in meaning; echo positive is and use it." data-fix="Nothing is missing, is it?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The old projector never works reliably, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="does it" data-hint="Never makes the lexical Present Simple statement negative; supply positive does." data-fix="The old projector never works reliably, does it?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The guests hardly ever request paper copies, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="do they" data-hint="Hardly ever carries negative meaning; supply positive do for the plural guests." data-fix="The guests hardly ever request paper copies, do they?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Learn the high-value special patterns as complete chunks</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The positive <span class="ex">I am</span> pattern uses conventional <span class="ex">aren’t I?</span> in standard conversation: <span class="ex">I’m next, aren’t I?</span> Negative <span class="ex">I’m not</span> takes regular positive <span class="ex">am I?</span> A suggestion with <span class="ex">Let’s</span> commonly takes <span class="ex">shall we?</span>. Imperatives commonly take <span class="ex">will you?</span>, although <span class="ex">would you, can you</span>, and <span class="ex">could you</span> change tone and request style.</p>
  <p>Imperative tags do not follow the simple opposite-polarity rule, so learn the whole chunk. Same-polarity conversational tags also exist, as in <span class="ex">So you finished, did you?</span>, but they carry other attitudes and are not the neutral confirmation pattern practiced here.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used aren’t I after positive I am, am I after I’m not, shall we after Let’s, and will you with both positive and negative imperatives.">
    <h3>Special-pattern check: retrieve the whole chunk</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Special question tag choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="aren’t I">aren’t I</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="am I">am I</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="shall we">shall we</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will you">will you</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Positive <span class="ex">I am</span>: I’m introducing the speaker, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="aren’t I" data-hint="Use the conventional standard tag aren’t I after positive I’m." data-fix="I’m introducing the speaker, aren’t I?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Negative <span class="ex">I am</span>: I’m not speaking too quickly, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="am I" data-hint="Reverse negative I’m not to positive am I." data-fix="I’m not speaking too quickly, am I?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Joint suggestion: Let’s send the Wi-Fi code now, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="shall we" data-hint="Let’s commonly takes the fixed tag shall we." data-fix="Let’s send the Wi-Fi code now, shall we?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Positive imperative with the lesson’s core tag: Please close the door, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will you" data-hint="The controlled imperative pattern uses will you here; actual tone depends on voice and relationship." data-fix="Please close the door, will you?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Negative imperative: Don’t forget the guest list, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will you" data-hint="A negative imperative can also take will you." data-fix="Don’t forget the guest list, will you?">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Answer the statement, and choose intonation from the situation</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A short answer responds to whether the statement is true, not whether the tag itself looks positive or negative. <span class="ex">You haven’t sent it, have you?</span> receives <span class="ex">No, I haven’t</span> when the negative statement is true, and <span class="ex">Yes, I have</span> when the listener corrects it.</p>
  <p>A falling tag often presents the speaker as fairly sure and invites alignment. A rising tag often makes the check more open. These are useful tendencies, not automatic translations of pitch into certainty. Variety, relationship, emotion, and discourse can change the effect. A falling tag may sound warm, routine, challenging, or pressuring. When genuine choice matters, ask a neutral full question such as <span class="ex">Does Tuesday work for you?</span></p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You answered the statement’s truth, matched falling and rising voice to the supplied certainty, and replaced a pressuring assumption with a neutral full question.">
    <h3>Social-meaning check: use the stated relationship and knowledge</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Question tag response and intonation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="No, I haven’t">No, I haven’t</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Yes, I have">Yes, I have</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a falling voice">a falling voice</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a rising voice">a rising voice</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Does Tuesday work for you?">Does Tuesday work for you?</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The code is still unsent. Answer <span class="ex">You haven’t sent it, have you?</span> with <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="No, I haven’t" data-hint="The negative statement is true, so confirm it with No and a negative short answer." data-fix="No, I haven’t.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The code was sent privately, so the negative statement is false. Correct it with <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Yes, I have" data-hint="Use Yes and a positive short answer because the code has been sent." data-fix="Yes, I have.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Lena is reading a verified 5:30 line and expects routine agreement. In this supplied context, she uses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a falling voice" data-hint="A fall often fits an expected confirmation in a routine cooperative exchange." data-fix="She uses a falling voice in this context.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Marcus sees a blank task but does not know whether Lena completed it elsewhere. For a more open check here, he uses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a rising voice" data-hint="A rise often fits a genuine information check when the speaker is uncertain." data-fix="He uses a rising voice for the open check here.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A colleague must be free to reject a proposed date. Replace <span class="ex">Tuesday is fine, isn’t it?</span> with the neutral full question <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Does Tuesday work for you?" data-hint="Ask directly about the colleague’s availability without embedding the preferred answer." data-fix="Does Tuesday work for you?">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: keep the tag as a short intonation unit</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A tag often forms a short intonation unit after the statement. The voice commonly falls through the end of an expected-confirmation tag and rises through the end of a more open check. The pitch movement belongs to the whole tag; do not assume that one fixed auxiliary or pronoun must always carry the strongest stress.</p>
  <p>In connected U.S. speech, <span class="ex">don’t you</span> may link toward /doʊntʃə/, and <span class="ex">didn’t you</span> may link toward /dɪdən(t)ʃə/. <span class="ex">Isn’t it</span> often compresses toward /ˈɪzənɪt/. These reductions must preserve the operator, polarity, and subject reference. In careful or contrastive speech, more of each word remains audible: <span class="ex">I said she HAS arrived, HASN’T she?</span></p>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read expected agreement, open confirmation, U.S. tag linking, negative-statement response, and contrastive operator listening check">
    <details class="reveal-card">
      <summary>Open the tutor listening check</summary>
